  "OverallSummary": "This case involves failures by Kayan Securities and its CEO/CCO Yong Soo Kim related to supervision of excessive trading and unauthorized trading, affecting three customers. From January 2018 through April 2021, the firm failed to establish, maintain, and enforce a reasonable supervisory system to monitor for excessive trading, initially lacking clear standards for identifying problematic trading. Even after implementing turnover rate and commission-to-equity benchmarks in their WSPs, their customer notification process was inadequate. After Regulation BI took effect on June 30, 2020, the firm also violated the Compliance Obligation by failing to update its procedures specifically for Reg BI compliance regarding excessive trading. The firm and Kim failed to reasonably respond to multiple red flags of Representative 1's unauthorized and excessive trading in three customers' accounts, including turnover rates above 6, pattern day trading designations, in-and-out trading patterns, losses of nearly $400,000 for Customer 1, and customer complaints. For Customer 2, a senior investor whose unauthorized trading occurred after Reg BI's effective date, the Care Obligation was violated through excessive trading. The firm also failed to report two customer complaints to FINRA and failed to update Representative 1's Form U4 and U5 with disclosure of three customer complaints. As sanctions, Kayan received a censure and must pay $50,000 in partial restitution to Customer 1 (paid in installments due to financial condition), while Kim received a two-month principal capacity suspension, a $5,000 fine, and must complete 20 hours of continuing education on supervisory responsibilities."
